In this sprint my goal was to finish the simulated combat sequence that I started in sprint 1. I am fully aware that I didn't do the post mortem to explain what I needed to do. However, from the moment I heard that we'd be expanding upon our first project, I knew exactly what I wanted to do. For this sprint I simulated a single battle instance in the game Fire Emblem Engage. The battle starts (e) and that player unit attacks (animations and such to be added in a later sprint). Then the enemy will take damage on, which can be seen on the visible HP indicator. After a lttle, the enemy will attack the player, dealing damage back. This is where you can see my mechanic in full effect. If the enemy is broken from the player's attack, they will not attack the player back. Originally, I had wanted to have more indicators for certain aspects on the screen, so that it would be easy to tell exactly what's going on. Unfortunately, I had to cut back on those due to the unforseen difficulty of adding in what I did. While coding this sprint, I ran into a lot of unfortunate and small issues that came from my lack of experience in c#. For the most part though, I was able to swiftly solve the issue and learn what caused it with the help of a peer.
